### What does collecting into a Set achieve?

A `Set` stores each element only once, determined by `equals` and `hashCode`. Collecting names into a set automatically removes duplicate names without needing an explicit `distinct()` call. The result is the set of unique names.

### How do you implement it?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CollectToSet {

    record Employee(String id, String name, String department, BigDecimal salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("SAV2001", "Rajesh",  "TRADING",    new BigDecimal("1800000")),
            new Employee("SAV2002", "Priya",   "RISK",       new BigDecimal("1200000")),
            new Employee("SAV2003", "Rajesh",  "OPERATIONS", new BigDecimal("900000")),
            new Employee("SAV2004", "Meera",   "TRADING",    new BigDecimal("1500000")),
            new Employee("SAV2005", "Priya",   "COMPLIANCE", new BigDecimal("1050000"))
        );

        Set<String> uniqueNames = staff.stream()
            .map(Employee::name)
            .collect(Collectors.toSet());

        System.out.println("Unique names: " + uniqueNames);
        System.out.println("Count: " + uniqueNames.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Unique names: [Meera, Priya, Rajesh]
Count: 3
```

### How do you control the set type and ordering?

```java
// Sorted (TreeSet): alphabetical order
TreeSet<String> sorted = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::name)
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(TreeSet::new));
// [Meera, Priya, Rajesh]

// Insertion order (LinkedHashSet): first-seen order
LinkedHashSet<String> insertionOrder = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::name)
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new));
// [Rajesh, Priya, Meera] – first occurrence order
```

### How is toSet() different from distinct().toList()?

| Approach | Result type | Uniqueness | Order |
|---|---|---|---|
| `toSet()` | `Set<String>` | By set contract | Unordered (HashSet) |
| `distinct().toList()` | `List<String>` | By distinct + equals | Encounter order preserved |
| `toCollection(TreeSet::new)` | `TreeSet<String>` | By comparator | Sorted |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Assuming `toSet()` preserves insertion order.

```java
// WRONG assumption: order is preserved
Set<String> names = staff.stream().map(Employee::name).collect(Collectors.toSet());
// Iteration order is unpredictable (HashSet)

// CORRECT for insertion order
LinkedHashSet<String> ordered = staff.stream()
    .map(Employee::name)
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new));
```

### What about custom objects and equals/hashCode?

For custom objects, deduplication depends entirely on correct `equals` and `hashCode`. If those are wrong, the set will not deduplicate as expected.

```java
// Records automatically generate equals/hashCode from all components
record AccountId(String branch, int number) {}

Set<AccountId> uniqueAccounts = transactions.stream()
    .map(Transaction::accountId)
    .collect(Collectors.toSet());
// Correct deduplication because record equals checks all fields
```

### What about null elements?

`HashSet` allows one `null` element, but `TreeSet` throws `NullPointerException` because it needs to compare elements. Filter nulls before collecting into a `TreeSet`.

### What should a senior engineer know?

`toSet()` gives no ordering guarantee and no control over the concrete type. When ordering or a specific set type matters, use `toCollection` with a supplier. For custom objects, verify that `equals` and `hashCode` are correct before relying on set deduplication.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you get a sorted set?" Use `toCollection(TreeSet::new)`.
- "What if equals/hashCode are wrong?" The set will not deduplicate correctly.
- "How is toSet different from distinct?" `toSet` returns a `Set`; `distinct` returns a `Stream` that can be collected into any structure.
